  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Quiet weather period with warming trend next\nweek.\n\nSmall bands of flurries/rain may continue into the afternoon \nwith little to no accumulation in the western mountains. There \nmay be another round of flurries overnight tonight for the \nmountains but totals are forecast to be trivial. Going into \nnext week a column of high pressure systems will dominate the \neastern half of the United States with a center of a surface \nhigh over the Mid-Atlantic. This will result in mostly clear \nskies and calm winds for the majority of next week. The other \nreal change in the weather pattern will be at the 500mb level \nwhere a Rex Block temporarily forms over the southeastern CONUS \nbefore the high component is squeezed out from a trough that \npasses to our north later in the week. The presence of the high,\nhowever, will result in increasing heights and a warming trend \nthat will provide some abnormally warm temperatures for this \ntime of year. For perspective, the highs for Sunday will be in \nthe lower 40s to upper 50s and then in the 60s to lower 70s by \nThursday. With the clear skies and calm winds, temperatures will\ncool off considerably each night with some lows in the 20s and \ngradually \"warming\" into the 30s.",
